{
  "requirement": "Source providers can override parsing, text search, and structural search.\nThe SDK supplies defaults for all three, so listing, reading, and a supplied\nlanguage registry suffice to support them.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.sources.provider_defaults`.\n\n**Obligations derived from the text**\n1. The SDK supplies a default for parsing, for text search and for structural search.\n2. A provider with only listing and reading, plus a supplied language registry, is enough to support all three.\n3. A provider can override parsing, text search and structural search. \"Can\" is permissive, so this needs no test beyond showing that overrides are honoured.\n\n**Test mapping**\n- **Default parse and default text search from list and read only.** `tests/sources.rs#fn[defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot]` uses `Memory`, whose `SourceProvider` impl defines only `list` and `read`.\n  - `parse` is called with a registry. The test asserts the text, the outline built from that text, and that exactly one read happened.\n  - `search_text` is called with a scope, a pattern and a context line count. The test asserts `complete`, the match count, the line number and the excerpt.\n  - The test asserts the exact event order of reads and listing, so the defaults provably go through the primitives.\n- **Default structural search from list and read only.** `tests/sources.rs#fn[list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search]` uses `Primitives`, which again defines only `list` and `read`.\n  - `parse` uses the language the provider reports for the file, and an explicit language wins over the file extension.\n  - `search_structure` finds `call()` in `fn[provided]` and returns the correct resource and excerpt.\n  - A malformed rule returns an error.\n  - A language that does not match the file gives an empty, complete result.\n  - An unknown language returns an error.\n  - A mismatched resource returned by an overridden parse is rejected.\n- **Overrides are honoured.** `tests/sources.rs#fn[structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope]` uses `CachedParse`, which overrides `parse`. Its `read` always fails, so any refetch would break the test.\n  - The test asserts the match results, the excerpt and the limit and completeness behaviour for `None`, `Some(1)` and `Some(0)`.\n  - It asserts that the override ran exactly 3 times, once per query. The default structural search therefore uses the overridden parse.\n- **Integration.** `tests/agent.rs#fn[source_tools_keep_search_discovery_separate_from_delivered_source_reads]` runs the default text search through `LocalSources` in the agent tools and checks the resulting observations.\n\n**Gaps**\nNo test overrides `search_text` on its own. The requirement only says a provider \"can\" do this, which is a permission, so it adds no obligation to test. The provider-level override mechanism is the same trait mechanism that the `parse` override test already exercises.\n\nThe `provider_defaults.composition` sub-requirement is covered by the same tests and is not assessed separately here.",
